<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuroda Yoshitaka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1546–1604</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Kuroda Kanbei, Kuroda Josui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuroda Yoshitaka, better known as Kuroda Kanbei, was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> and strategist who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> in his campaigns to unify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and was rewarded with lands in Buzen province. A convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, he later retired as the lay monk Josui. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> campaign of 1600 he fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagamasa">Kuroda Nagamasa</a> became the first lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> domain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuroda-yoshitaka.jpg" alt="Kuroda Yoshitaka" width="320" height="704" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yoshitaka_Kuroda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
